  "title": "A smoother server for big crowds",
  "body": "- Busy servers run much smoother: the minimap, the story tracker, inventory pages, Easy Building and the townsfolk name labels now cost the server a small part of what they did. With many players moving at once the minimap may update a moment later; players outside your party show within 128 blocks.\n- Expansive Inventory pages now work in the Nether and the End.\n- A dish in the cooking pot is no longer lost if the server restarts while it cooks.\n- Tornadoes no longer drop you to your death: Slow Falling now lasts until you land.\n- Expeditions: the clock counts game time, so lag no longer eats your time. Turned away because every dungeon was busy? You get a message when one is free.\n- Hearth and Table can be finished without the Kitchen or Champions pack: Bea sends you after Grandmother Gristle. Story build steps count every color of candle, glass and sapling.\n- In a party, Track this quest also works on a quest you already track, and wave notes no longer replace the quest box.\n- Parties, community goals and tamed dolphins keep saving however many a big server has. Realm Mail: type the name of anyone not in the To list.\n- Nicknames can't copy an offline player's gamertag. Closing My preferences with X just says so in chat. Lag Cleanup keeps dropped bundles. A Stormcaller's lightning no longer strikes inside safe zones, claims or towns.\n- The town talks about more now: reached goals, top milestones, bounties, treasure, caravans, tornado rides and storm hauls. Goal donors get thank-you letters, and there are new titles (Pillar of ..., Miner III, Bounty Hunter). A Blood Moon is one piece of news now, not one per survivor.\n- Away when a town project was finished? The town sends you a letter saying your Crowns wait for you.\n- Operators: Set up this world (Operator Tools, or /realm:admin_setup) walks a new world's setup step by step. Server size switches the settings that matter between a small Realm and a big server in one go, after showing every change.\n- Operators: Pack diagnostics (Operator Tools, or /realm:diag) shows how much script time and saved data each pack uses, and can make one pack forget its saved data before you remove it."
